  THE LONELY RUNNER CONJECTURE FOR SIX RUNNERS — FORMAL VERIFICATION IN LEAN 4
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  Self-contained, machine-checkable Lean 4 formalization of the Lonely Runner
  Conjecture in the case of six runners (five moving runners plus a stationary
  observer; `n = 6` in the normalisation below, `n = 5` in the labelling used
  by part of the literature). The cases of three, four, and five runners are
  included as well, since the six-runner assembly uses them.

  MAIN RESULT (the final theorem of this file):

    theorem intLRC_six : intLRC 6

  where (definitions in Part 1 below):

    def intLRC (n : ℕ) : Prop :=
      ∀ (speeds : Fin (n - 1) → ℕ),
        StrictMono speeds →
        (∀ i, 0 < speeds i) →
        Finset.gcd Finset.univ speeds = 1 →
        ∃ t : ℝ, ∀ i, circDist ((speeds i : ℝ) * t) ≥ (1 : ℝ) / (n : ℝ)

    def circDist (x : ℝ) : ℝ := min (Int.fract x) (1 - Int.fract x)

  In words: for any 5 distinct positive integer speeds with gcd 1 there is a
  time `t` at which every runner is at circle-distance at least 1/6 from the
  stationary observer. The reduction from arbitrary (real, possibly negative,
  possibly non-normalised) speeds to this integer form is the standard one in
  the literature and is not part of this file.

